Return concrete types from the green helpers

The lazy eventlet import left futurist/_green.py exposing Pool, Queue and
spawn as module attributes served by a module-level __getattr__. That
works, but it costs the type checker everything it knew about them: the
attributes are annotated Any, so GreenThreadPoolExecutor has to annotate
its own _pool and _delayed_work as Any too, and a module that defines
__getattr__ accepts any attribute at all, so a misspelled _green.Queu is
only a runtime AttributeError.

_green is internal, so nothing outside futurist depends on those
attributes being attributes. Replace them with functools.cache'd
accessors -- get_pool_class(), get_queue_class() and get_spawn() --
annotated with the eventlet types they return. Nothing is imported until
the first call, as before, and the cache makes that import happen once.
eventlet ships no type information, so each accessor carries one
no-any-return ignore; the point is that the Any now stops there instead
of reaching the callers.

Also stop GreenThreading being optional. It was GreenThreading | None,
which bought nothing: constructing it imports nothing, and its methods
raise when eventlet is missing. Non-optional removes two "is None"
guards and two asserts from _futures.py. The four duplicated imports of
eventlet.green.threading become one cached helper, which is also the one
place that reports a missing eventlet for those primitives.

No behaviour change beyond the error raised when eventlet is absent,
which was RuntimeError before and still is, with a message naming what
was wanted rather than the mixin that wanted it.

What this does not fix: SynchronousExecutor still assigns _green.threading
over an attribute typed as _thread.Threading, so that line keeps its
ignore[assignment]. Giving the two threading facades a shared Protocol is
the next step, and is left for its own change.

Prompt: Stephen Finucane asked on the parent change for the class
attributes to become cached methods returning concrete types, as a
follow-up. Mikal asked for it as a separate patch layered into the
series, partly so the shape of the change is easy to read.

